@node	0.7, 0.8, 0.6, Chapter 0
@section Testing

This section covers the test framework, property tests, how solutions are
checked, and the pending marker. The rig is small on purpose: Kotest
specifications for structure and assertions, generated inputs where a
property beats an example, and one recipe that reports what is still
unsolved.

@heading Anatomy of a spec

A test file is a Kotlin class extending Kotest's @code{FunSpec}. Each test
is registered by name inside the specification block and asserts with
matchers such as @code{shouldBe}. Table-style tests register one test per
row by looping over the rows when the specification is built; Kotest's
@code{withData} helper does the same job and lives in a module this edition
does not pin, so the book's listings write the loop out:

@example kotlin
public fun parity(n: Long): String = if (n % 2L == 0L) "even" else "odd"

public class S0_7TestingTest :
    FunSpec({
        listOf(
            0L to "even",
            1L to "odd",
            42L to "even",
        ).forEach { (n, expected) ->
            test("parity($n) is $expected") {
                parity(n) shouldBe expected
            }
        }
    })
@end example

@noindent
A property test states what must hold for every generated input and lets
the framework try to break it. @code{checkAll} draws values from an
@code{Arb} generator, shrinks a failing sample to a minimal case, and
reports the first counterexample. Boundary cases that generation would
rarely hit stay explicit:

@example kotlin
test("a property test runs the assertion over generated inputs") {
    checkAll(Arb.long(-100L, 100L), Arb.long(-100L, 100L)) { a, b ->
        absDiff(a, b) shouldBe absDiff(b, a)
    }
}

test("boundary cases stay explicit") {
    absDiff(0L, 0L) shouldBe 0L
    absDiff(Long.MAX_VALUE, Long.MAX_VALUE) shouldBe 0L
}
@end example

@noindent
Kotest test bodies may suspend, so the concurrent code of @ref{3.4} tests
directly; chapter 3 additionally pins @file{kotlinx-coroutines-test} for
the cases that need virtual time. Where the book needs randomness, it does
not reach for the host generator: the runtime ships a seeded
@code{Random}, so the stochastic exercises of @ref{1.2} and @ref{3.1} give
the same transcript on every machine and every run.

@heading How solutions are checked

Exercises and solutions share fully qualified names, and a test run
receives exactly one of the two on its classpath: each chapter registers
three test tasks, @code{examplesTest}, @code{exercisesTest}, and
@code{solutionsTest}, each carrying the runtime classpath of its own
source set, so no run ever mixes them. The task you run chooses, not a
flag. Day to day:

@example
just test       # examples and solutions tests, the green gate
just scaffold   # exercisesTest --continue: the pending report
@end example

@noindent
@command{just scaffold} runs every exercise's disabled-enabled pair. A
solved exercise's test runs for real; an unsolved one stays disabled with
its reason, and the recipe exits nonzero while anything is pending, which
makes the pending list a report rather than a broken build. The marker the
scaffold throws is the runtime's @code{PendingSolution}, and the disabled
test is what keeps the gate green until the solution lands.

The full gate is @command{just lint}: @code{./gradlew build}, which
compiles the runtime and the four source sets of every chapter, applies
@command{ktlint} 1.8.0, and fails on any compiler warning, because the
build sets @code{allWarningsAsErrors}. The file names that carry section
numbers, like @code{E1_10Ackermann.kt}, are why the stock ktlint naming
rules are switched off project-wide.

